# maspsx fix: generalize the `.section` directive recognized in the sdata
# pre-scan (w26-a3, TOOLCHAIN wave, 2026-07-25).
#
# SYMPTOM: compiling libgpu/SYS.cpp (or any other C-lane TU using this
# tree's `__attribute__((section(".data")))` / `__attribute__((section(
# ".bss")))` "force absolute placement" lever, §3.12) through CC1PSX and
# piping its .s straight to maspsx crashes:
#
#     MASPSX: An exception occurred: Unable to parse .sdata instruction:
#     .section .data,"aw",@progbits
#
# ROOT CAUSE: maspsx's `preprocess_lines` pre-scan (which sizes every
# `.sdata`-region symbol so the real emission pass can later decide
# `.sbss` vs `.bss`) tracks an `in_sdata` flag. It sets `in_sdata = True`
# on a bare `.sdata` directive and clears it again on `.text`/`.data`/
# `.rdata`/`.file`/`.extern`/`.comm`/`.lcomm`, and (already) on a bare
# `.section .text`. But CC1PSX ALSO emits the generic ELF-style
# `.section NAME,"FLAGS",@TYPE` form -- confirmed present verbatim inside
# CC1PSX.EXE as the literal format strings `.section %s,"ax",@progbits`
# (executable), `"a",@progbits` (read-only) and `"aw",@progbits`
# (writable) -- for ANY global carrying an explicit `__attribute__((
# section(...)))`. That form doesn't match `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata`/
# `.sdata`/`.file`/`.extern`/`.comm`/`.lcomm`, and the old check only
# matched a `.section` line ending in the literal string `.text`. So a
# `.section .data,...`/`.section .bss,...` line left `in_sdata` however it
# was left by whatever came before it. When it followed a bare `.sdata`
# region with NO intervening `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata` reset, `in_sdata`
# was still True, the line fell through every recognized-directive branch,
# and the pre-scan's `if in_sdata:` catch-all (which only knows `.size`/
# a label/`.type`/`.space`/`.word`/`.half`/`.short`/`.byte`/`.ascii`)
# raised.
#
# REAL TRIGGER (libgpu/SYS.cpp, confirmed by compiling the file's global
# declarations through the real CC1PSX -- `_gpu_tbl` (a `static const`
# driver-dispatch struct) lands in `.sdata` under this compiler's
# `-mgpOPT`; its `"GPU"` id string's `$LC1` literal-pool entry is the LAST
# thing in that `.sdata` run; the very next global, `GEnv_drv`, carries
# `__attribute__((section(".data")))` (§3.12 "force absolute placement" --
# without it a 4-byte initialized pointer would default into .sdata under
# -G4, a gp-relative-load divergence vs the oracle) and is emitted as
# `.section .data,"aw",@progbits` with NO `.text` in between:
#
#     .globl  _gpu_tbl
#     .sdata                              <- in_sdata = True
#     .align  2
#     _gpu_tbl:
#     .word   $LC1
#     .align  2
#     $LC1:
#     .ascii  "GPU\000"
#     .globl  GEnv_drv
#     .section .data,"aw",@progbits       <- CRASH here (unpatched)
#     .align  2
#     GEnv_drv:
#     .word   _gpu_tbl
#
# FIX: recognize ANY `.section` directive (not just the `.text`-suffixed
# one), parse its section NAME, and treat it exactly like the matching
# bare directive would: `sdata`/`sbss` (re-)enter the sdata scan (parity
# with bare `.sdata`, in case a future TU ever forces `__attribute__((
# section(".sdata")))`); anything else (`.text`, `.data`, `.bss`,
# `.rodata`/`.rdata`, or an arbitrary named section) resets it -- same
# semantics the existing bare `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata` branches already
# have. This only changes the PRE-SCAN's bookkeeping of `in_sdata`; the
# real emission pass (`process_line`) already passes an unrecognized
# `.section NAME,"FLAGS",@TYPE` line through UNCHANGED (its `if line.
# startswith(".")` chain falls to the `else: res.append(line)` case), so
# GNU `as` -- which understands the ELF section syntax natively -- still
# assembles these forced-placement globals correctly; nothing about their
# actual output section changes, only whether the pre-scan can get past
# them without crashing.
#
# VERIFIED (2026-07-25, isolated copy C:/Temp/maspsx-a3/ first, per house
# rule -- NOT applied to the shared C:/Temp/maspsx-master/ until proven):
#   - maspsx's own `tests/` unittest suite: 136/136 pass unpatched-behavior-
#     preserving, plus 3 new regression cases added to `tests/test_directives.py`
#     (`test_named_section_resets_sdata_scan`, `test_named_bss_section_after_
#     sdata_run`, `test_named_sdata_section_enters_sdata_scan`) -- 139/139
#     pass on the patched copy; the crash-reproducing case raises on an
#     UNPATCHED copy (confirmed) and passes clean on the patched one.
#   - Full-file diagnostic: CC1PSX's real (error-laden but still section-
#     emitting) .s for libgpu/SYS.cpp with `extern "C"` mechanically
#     stripped (a throwaway C89-migration probe, not the real migration)
#     crashes maspsx unpatched with the exact reported error, and processes
#     cleanly (RC=0) patched, byte-identical whether run via the isolated
#     copy or the deployed shared master.
#   - Regression, 0 change to already-working output (compiled + objdump'd
#     .o BYTE-IDENTICAL between unpatched master and the patched copy) for
#     6 functions spanning 4 different clusters plus the jtbl-fusion and
#     per-function delayed-branch-splice code paths (so this fix cannot be
#     interacting badly with either mechanism):
#       atoi              (libc/C16.c)              -- known PASS
#       DeliverEvent      (libapi/A07.c)             -- known PASS
#       CdSync            (libcd/cdcont.c)           -- known PASS, spliced (PER_FN_NO_DELAYED_BRANCH)
#       PadStartCom       (libpad/PADENTRY.c)        -- known PASS, spliced (PER_FN_NO_DELAYED_BRANCH)
#       PadInfoAct        (libpad/PADENTRY.c)        -- jtbl-fusion (PER_TU_FLAGS)
#       _cdread2_ready    (libcd/cdread2.c)          -- known PASS, spliced (PER_FN_NO_DELAYED_BRANCH)
#
# APPLY: copy C:/Temp/maspsx-master/ to an isolated working copy (do NOT
# edit the shared master directly -- other agents build against it
# concurrently), apply this patch there, prove it (as above), THEN copy
# the same two files over the shared master and refresh a dated backup
# (this session: C:/Temp/maspsx-master-backup-presection/, alongside the
# pre-existing C:/Temp/maspsx-master-backup-prejtbl/) before overwriting.
#
# --- a/maspsx/__init__.py -----------------------------------------------
--- a/maspsx/__init__.py
+++ b/maspsx/__init__.py
@@ -582,8 +582,34 @@
                 in_sdata = False
                 continue

-            if line.startswith(".section") and line.endswith(".text"):
-                in_sdata = False
+            if line.startswith(".section"):
+                # w26-a3: generic ELF-style `.section NAME[,"FLAGS",@TYPE]` directive,
+                # not just the bare `.section .text` this used to special-case. CC1PSX
+                # emits this form (via GCC's generic named_section() output path,
+                # confirmed present as literal `.section %s,"aw",@progbits` /
+                # `"a",@progbits` / `"ax",@progbits" format strings inside CC1PSX.EXE)
+                # for any global carrying an explicit
+                # `__attribute__((section(".data"/".bss"/...)))` -- used throughout this
+                # tree (§3.12 "force absolute placement" lever) to pin a would-be-.sdata/
+                # .sbss global into regular .data/.bss so it addresses absolute instead
+                # of gp-relative, matching the retail oracle. The narrow `endswith(".text")`
+                # check here only recognised `.section .text`; any OTHER named section
+                # (`.section .data,"aw",@progbits`, `.section .bss,"aw",@progbits`, ...)
+                # fell through every branch below and -- if a prior bare `.sdata` block
+                # had left `in_sdata` True with no intervening `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata` --
+                # hit the catch-all "Unable to parse .sdata instruction" exception in the
+                # `in_sdata` scan below. (Real trigger, libgpu/SYS.cpp: a `.sdata`-placed
+                # const table's literal-pool tail is immediately followed by
+                # `GEnv_drv`'s forced `.section .data,"aw",@progbits`, no `.text` between.)
+                # Fix: parse the section NAME out of any `.section` directive and treat it
+                # exactly like the matching bare directive would -- `sdata`/`sbss` (re-)enter
+                # the sdata scan (parity with bare `.sdata`, in case a future TU ever forces
+                # `__attribute__((section(".sdata")))`), anything else (`.text`, `.data`,
+                # `.bss`, `.rodata`/`.rdata`, or an arbitrary named section) resets it, same
+                # as the existing bare `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata` handling above.
+                m = re.match(r"^\.section\s+\.?([A-Za-z0-9_.]+)", line)
+                name = m.group(1) if m else ""
+                in_sdata = name in ("sdata", "sbss")
                 continue

             if line.startswith("#"):
--- a/tests/test_directives.py
+++ b/tests/test_directives.py
@@ -18,3 +18,64 @@
         res = mp.process_line(line)
         self.assertEqual([line], res)
         self.assertEqual(mp.file_num, 2)
+
+    def test_named_section_resets_sdata_scan(self):
+        # w26-a3: CC1PSX emits the generic ELF `.section NAME,"FLAGS",@TYPE` form for
+        # any global carrying an explicit `__attribute__((section(".data"/".bss"/...)))`
+        # (used to pin an otherwise-.sdata/.sbss-eligible global into a regular, absolute
+        # section). Previously only a bare `.section .text` was recognised as ending an
+        # `.sdata` run; any OTHER named section immediately after a bare `.sdata` block
+        # (with no intervening `.text`/`.data`/`.rdata`) fell through to the sdata-entry
+        # scan's catch-all and raised "Unable to parse .sdata instruction". Real trigger
+        # (libgpu/SYS.cpp): a `.sdata`-placed const table's ascii literal-pool tail
+        # immediately followed by an attribute-forced `.section .data,"aw",@progbits`.
+        lines = [
+            ".sdata",
+            ".align\t2",
+            "tbl:",
+            ".word\t$LC1",
+            ".align\t2",
+            "$LC1:",
+            '.ascii\t"GPU\\000"',
+            ".globl\tGEnv_drv",
+            '.section .data,"aw",@progbits',
+            ".align\t2",
+            "GEnv_drv:",
+            ".word\ttbl",
+        ]
+        mp = MaspsxProcessor(lines)
+        # must not raise "Unable to parse .sdata instruction: .section .data,..."
+        out = mp.process_lines()
+        self.assertIn("GEnv_drv:", out)
+
+    def test_named_bss_section_after_sdata_run(self):
+        # Same bug, `.bss`-flavoured (the other section name this tree's
+        # `__attribute__((section(".bss")))` globals use).
+        lines = [
+            ".sdata",
+            ".align\t2",
+            "small_const:",
+            ".word\t5",
+            ".globl\tGPU_GP0",
+            '.section .bss,"aw",@progbits',
+            ".align\t2",
+            "GPU_GP0:",
+            ".space\t4",
+        ]
+        mp = MaspsxProcessor(lines)
+        out = mp.process_lines()
+        self.assertIn("GPU_GP0:", out)
+
+    def test_named_sdata_section_enters_sdata_scan(self):
+        # An explicit `.section .sdata,...` (never emitted by this project's CC1PSX in
+        # practice, but the general form) should behave like the bare `.sdata` directive
+        # -- i.e. still be tracked by the sdata-entry scan -- not silently ignored.
+        lines = [
+            '.section .sdata,"aw",@progbits',
+            ".align\t2",
+            "small:",
+            ".word\t1",
+        ]
+        mp = MaspsxProcessor(lines)
+        out = mp.process_lines()
+        self.assertIn("small:", out)
